WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.800
In South Africa where I come from Seoul Bonnet is the Zulu word for hello. There's a beautiful

2
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.840
and powerful intention behind the word because South Bonnet literally translated means I see you

3
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:38.160
and by seeing you I bring you into being. So beautiful imagine being greeted like that.

4
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.040
But what does it take in the way we see ourselves, our thoughts, our emotions and our stories

5
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.160
that help us to thrive in an increasingly complex and fraught world. This crucial question has

6
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.600
been at the center of my life's work because how we deal with our inner world drives everything,

7
00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.680
every aspect of how we love, how we live, how we parent and how we lead. The conventional view

8
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:14.240
of emotions as good or bad, positive or negative is rigid and rigidity in the face of complexity is

9
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:21.200
toxic. We need greater levels of emotional agility for true resilience and thriving.

10
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:29.280
My journey with this calling began not in the hallowed halls of a university, but in the messy

11
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:36.000
tender business of life. I grew up in the white suburbs of apartheid South Africa. A country

12
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.840
and community committed to not seeing, to denial. It's denial that makes 50 years of racist legislation

13
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:51.360
possible while people convince themselves that they are doing nothing wrong. And yet I first learned

14
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.680
of the destructive power of denial at a personal level before I understood what it was doing to the

15
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:08.640
country of my birth. My father died on a Friday. He was 42 years old and I was 15. My mother whispered

16
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.600
me to go and say goodbye to my father before I went to school. So I put my backpack down and

17
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.400
walked the passage that ran through to where the heart of our home, my father lay dying of cancer.

18
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.200
His eyes were closed but he knew I was there. In his presence, I had always felt seen.

19
00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.240
I told him I loved him, said goodbye and headed off for my day.

20
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.440
At school, I drifted from science to mathematics to history to biology as my father slipped from the

21
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:44.800
world. From May to July to September to November, I went about with my usual smile. I didn't drop a

22
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:52.640
single grade. When asked how I was doing, I would shrug and say, okay, I was praised for being

23
00:02:52.640 --> 00:03:01.280
strong. I was the master of being okay. But back home, we struggled. My father hadn't been able to

24
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.400
keep his small business going during his illness. And my mother, Lauren, was grieving the love of her

25
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.560
life, trying to raise three children and the creditors were knocking. We felt as a family

26
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:19.280
financially and emotionally ravaged and I began to spiral down isolated fast.

27
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:29.520
I started to use food to numb my pain, binging and purging, refusing to accept the full weight of my

28
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:36.560
grief. No one knew and in a culture that values relentless positivity, I thought that no one wanted to

29
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:46.240
know. But one person did not buy into my story of triumph over grief. My eighth grade English teacher

30
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:53.920
fixed me with burning blue eyes as she handed out blank notebooks. She said, write what you're feeling.

31
00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:02.720
Tell the truth. Write like nobody's reading. And just like that, I was invited to show

32
00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:10.000
authentically to my grief and pain. It was a simple act, but nothing short of a revolution for me.

33
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:19.280
It was this revolution that started in this blank notebook 30 years ago that shaped my life's work,

34
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:27.840
the secret silent correspondence with myself. Like a gymnast, I started to move beyond the

35
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.840
rigidity of denial into what I've now come to call emotional agility.

36
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:46.560
Life's beauty is inseparable from its fragility. We are young until we are not. We walk down

37
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:55.200
the street sexy until one day we realize that we are unseen. We nag our children and one day

38
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.440
realize that there is silence where their child once was now making his or her way in the world.

39
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:09.360
We are healthy until a diagnosis brings us to our knees. The only certainty is uncertainty,

40
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.720
and yet we are not navigating this frailty successfully or sustainably. The World Health

41
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.560
Organization tells us that depression is now the single leading cause of disability globally,

42
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:29.680
outstripping cancer, outstripping heart disease, and at a time of greater complexity,

43
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.560
unprecedented technological, political, and economic change. We are seeing how people's tendency

44
00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.400
is more and more to lock down into rigid responses to their emotions.

45
00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.200
On the one hand we might obsessively brood on our feelings,

46
00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:53.120
getting stuck inside our heads, hooked on being right, or victimized by our newsfeed.

47
00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.800
On the other we might bottle our emotions, pushing them aside, and permitting only those emotions

48
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:08.640
deemed legitimate. In a survey I recently conducted with over 70,000 people, I found that a third of us,

49
00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.360
a third, either judge ourselves for having so-called bad emotions,

50
00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:25.600
like sadness, anger, or even grief, or actively try to push aside these feelings.

51
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.240
We do this not only to ourselves, but also to people we live like our children. We may inadvertently

52
00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:40.240
shame them out of emotion seen as negative, jump to solution, and fail to help them to see

53
00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:49.760
these emotions as inherently valuable. Normal natural emotions are now seen as good or bad,

54
00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.480
and being positive has become a new form of moral correctness.

55
00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:02.800
People with cancer are automatically told to just stay positive.

56
00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:18.320
Woman to stop being so angry, and the list goes on, it's a tyranny, it's a tyranny of positivity,

57
00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:31.520
and it's cruel, unkind, and ineffective, and we do it ourselves, and we do it to others.

58
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.440
If there's one common feature of brooding, bottling, or forced positivity, it's this.

59
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:47.760
They are all rigid responses, and if there's a single lesson we can learn from the inevitable fall

60
00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:57.920
of apartheid, it is that rigid denial doesn't work. It's unsustainable for individuals, for families,

61
00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:07.360
for societies, and as we watch the ice caps melt, it is unsustainable for our planet.

62
00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.760
Research on emotional suppression shows that when emotions are pushed aside or ignored,

63
00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:18.800
they get stronger. Psychologists call this amplification,

64
00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.640
like that delicious chocolate cake in the refrigerator. The more you try to ignore it,

65
00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:37.520
the greater it's hold on you. You might think you're in control of unwanted emotions when you

66
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:47.680
ignore them, but in fact, they control you. Internal pain always comes out, always, and who pays the

67
00:08:47.680 --> 00:09:03.680
price, we do our children, our colleagues, our communities. Now don't get me wrong, I'm not

68
00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:12.320
anti-happiness. I like being happy. I'm a pretty happy person. But when we push aside normal

69
00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:19.680
emotions to embrace false positivity, we lose our capacity to develop skills to deal with the world

70
00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:27.760
as it is, not as we wish it to be. I've had hundreds of people tell me what they don't want to feel.

71
00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.600
They say things like, I don't want to try because I don't want to feel disappointed,

72
00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:45.760
or I just want this feeling to go away. I understand, I said to them, but you have dead people's goals.

73
00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:04.320
Only dead people never get unwanted or inconvenienced by their feelings.

74
00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:13.280
Only dead people never get stressed, never get broken hearts, never experience the disappointment

75
00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:23.040
that comes with failure. Tough emotions are part of our contract with life. You don't get to have

76
00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:30.240
a meaningful career, or raise a family, or leave the world a better place without stress and

77
00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:40.880
discomfort. Discomfort is the price of admission to a meaningful life. So how do we begin to

78
00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:48.800
dismantle rigidity and embrace emotional agility? As that young schoolgirl when I leaned into those

79
00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:56.240
blank pages, I started to do away with feelings of what I should be experiencing,

80
00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:06.960
and instead started to open my heart to what I did feel, pain and grief and loss and regret.

81
00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:17.040
Research now shows that the radical acceptance of all of our emotions, even the messy difficult

82
00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:23.120
ones, is the cornerstone to resilience, thriving and true authentic happiness.

83
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:31.680
But emotional agility is more than just an acceptance of emotions. We also know that accuracy

84
00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:38.880
matters. In my own research, I've found that words are essential. We often use quick and easy

85
00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:44.080
labels to describe our feelings. I'm stressed as the most common one I hear. But there's a world

86
00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:49.200
of difference between stress and disappointment or stress and that knowing dread of I'm in the

87
00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:55.600
wrong career. When we label our emotions accurately, we are more able to discern the precise

88
00:11:55.600 --> 00:12:01.200
cause of our feelings. And what scientists call the readiness potential in our brain is activated

89
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:07.920
allowing us to take concrete steps. But not just any steps, the right steps for us. Because our

90
00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:13.520
emotions are data, our emotions contain flashing lights to things that we care about.

91
00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:20.640
We tend not to feel strong emotion, to stuff that doesn't mean anything in our worlds.

92
00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:28.160
If you feel rage when you read the news, that rage is a signpost, perhaps, that you value equity

93
00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:33.440
and fairness, and an opportunity to take active steps to shape your life in that direction.

94
00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:40.080
When we are open to the difficult emotions, we are able to generate responses that are values

95
00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:47.520
aligned. But there's an important caveat. Emotions are data they are not directives. We can show up

96
00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:53.680
turn mind our emotions for their values without needing to listen to them. Just like I can show up

97
00:12:53.680 --> 00:13:00.960
to my son in his frustration with his baby sister. But not endorse his idea that he gets to give her

98
00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:08.000
a way to the first stranger he sees in a shopping mall. We own our emotions, they don't own us.

99
00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:14.640
When we internalize the difference between how I feel in all my wisdom and what I do in a values

100
00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:21.600
aligned action, we generate the pathway to our best selves via our emotions.

101
00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:30.960
So, what does this look like in practice? When you feel a strong tough emotion, don't race for

102
00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:38.960
the emotional exits. Learn its contours, show up to the journal of your hearts. What is the emotion telling

103
00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:46.720
you? And try not to say I am, as in I am angry or I am said, when you say I am, it makes you

104
00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:51.440
sound as if you are the emotion. Whereas you are you and the emotion is a data source.

105
00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:56.720
Instead try to notice the feeling for what it is. I'm noticing that I'm feeling said or I'm

106
00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:03.760
noticing that I'm feeling angry. These are essential skills for us, our families, our communities.

107
00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:10.560
They're also critical to the workplace. In my research, when I looked at what helps people to

108
00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:16.000
bring the best of themselves to work, I found a powerful key contributor, individualized consideration.

109
00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:22.880
When people are allowed to feel their emotional truth, engagement, creativity and innovation

110
00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:28.880
flourish in the organization. Diversity isn't just people, it's also what's inside people,

111
00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:39.120
including diversity of emotion. The most agile, resilient, individuals, teams, organizations,

112
00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:45.520
families, communities are built on an openness to the normal human emotions. It's this that allows us

113
00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:52.560
to say, what is my emotion telling me? Which action will bring me towards my values, which will take

114
00:14:52.560 --> 00:15:00.560
me away from my values? Emotional agility is the ability to be with your emotions, with curiosity,

115
00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:05.760
compassion, and especially the courage to take values connected steps.

116
00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:13.120
When I was little, I would wake up at night terrified by the idea of death. My father would comfort me

117
00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:20.480
with soft pets and kisses, but he would never lie. We all die, Susie, he would say,

118
00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:27.600
it's normal to be scared. He didn't try to invent a buffer between me and reality.

119
00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:33.200
It took me a while to understand the power of how he guided me through those nights.

120
00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:38.080
What he showed me is that courage is not an absence of fear.

121
00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:47.600
Courage is fear, walking. Neither of us knew that in ten short years he would be gone,

122
00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:56.000
and the time for each of us is all too precious and all too brief. But when our moment comes,

123
00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:06.320
to face our fragility in that ultimate time, it will ask us, are you agile? Are you agile?

124
00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:16.400
Let the moment be an unreserved yes, a yes born of a lifelong correspondence with your own heart,

125
00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:25.360
and in seeing yourself, because in seeing yourself, you are also able to see others too.

126
00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:33.120
The only sustainable way forward in a fragile, beautiful world.

127
00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:37.040
So well-borner, and thank you.
